he's underway early most days  several times a month javier ramirez  climbs the takanaw volcano on behalf of  iucn  the world's biggest environmental  organization  everything he encounters on his way up  fascinates him  it's the interaction of the different  species that make for this special  variety  starting with the animals the fungi the  bacteria and the plants  every day the volcano is shrouded in  clouds that cover the surrounding trees  and other plants  the clouds keep the ground moist and the  air humid  farmer frantisco ortiz owns land up here  that he got from the state  together with the conservationist he's  building barriers of old tree trunks to  protect deforested slopes from erosion  nature means everything to us because we  live from it  that's why it's important to us to  protect it  is on a slope of the volcano at an  altitude of 1700 meters  the people here live exclusively from  growing coffee  at this altitude coffee beans are more  aromatic earning more money for the  farmers  to protect his coffee plants and the  soil francisco ortiz has switched to  organic fertilizer  the organic fertilizer makes the coffee  plants stronger  where to use chemicals my plants would  perhaps thrive for a year or two but  then they'd die  the organic fertilizer makes my plants  more robust  but the farmers still can't prevent  disease from attacking their plants  diseases like the fungus known as coffee  rust or la roya  eventually this leaf will turn  completely yellow and die the entire  coffee plant will dry out and no longer  produce beans  many farmers on takanawa have switched  to sustainable farming without chemicals  like letizia flores who sells her  flowers at an organic market in  kakawatan  as part of an iucn initiative what's  good for nature is useful for the  farmers  the market is an additional source of  income for them  we come here to sell what little we have  to increase our income  and pay for school for our children  it's important for javier ramirez that  farmers benefit from environmental  protection  for them the fertile soil around takana  remains their most important resource  and they want to keep it that way for a  long time to come
 

